# 0001. Record architecture decisions as .adr files

- Status: Proposed
- Date: 2026-11-19

Decisions with long-term consequences for this project are recorded as numbered `.adr` files
in `docs/adr/`, so the why is in one place instead of spread over the notebook and README.

## Technical details
- **Problem**: the reasons behind the model and the Telegram bot lived in notebook markdown
  cells, the README and `CLAUDE.md`; the Telegram PR (#1) already used `[ADR-0000.1]`
  because there were no ADRs to point to.
- **Files**: `docs/adr/NNNN-short-title.adr`: sequential four-digit numbers, never reused;
  Markdown content with the `.adr` extension. `docs/adr/README.md` is the index.
- **Format**: the `adr-manager` template (title, Status, Date, purpose, Technical details,
  Technologies and alternatives). Statuses: Proposed, Accepted, Rejected, Deprecated,
  Superseded (linked both ways).
- **History**: 0002-0005 are recorded retroactively from `chatty_bot.ipynb`,
  `telegram_bot.py`, the README and commits `1ac3c62` and `8f50416` (#1).
- **Commits**: subjects carry the ADR they fall under (`[ADR-NNNN]`, minor `[ADR-NNNN.N]`,
  `commit-style`); `CLAUDE.md` says to read the ADR before changing a recorded decision.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Alternatives: keep the why in the notebook and README: mixed with usage docs, no status
  or supersession; `.md` extension: ADRs can't be told apart from other docs.
